207. Course Schedule

Approach: BFS + Topological Sorting (Kahn's Algorithm)

We use Kahn's Algorithm to determine whether all courses can be completed.

Build an adjacency list where an edge b → a represents that course b must be completed before course a.

Calculate the indegree of every vertex.

Push all courses with indegree 0 into a queue because they have no prerequisites.

Process the queue using BFS:

Remove a course and add it to the topological ordering.

Decrease the indegree of all its neighbouring courses.

If any neighbour's indegree becomes 0, push it into the queue.

Finally, check whether the number of processed courses equals numCourses.

If all courses are processed, return true; otherwise, a cycle exists, so return false.

Code

class Solution {
public:
    bool canFinish(int numCourses, vector<vector<int>>& prerequisites) {
        vector<vector<int>> edge(numCourses);
        vector<int> indegree(numCourses, 0);

        for(const auto& it : prerequisites) {
            edge[it[1]].push_back(it[0]);
            indegree[it[0]]++;
        }

        queue<int> q;

        for(int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++)
            if(indegree[i] == 0)
                q.push(i);

        vector<int> topo;

        while(!q.empty()) {
            int num = q.front();
            q.pop();

            topo.push_back(num);

            for(int neighbour : edge[num]) {
                indegree[neighbour]--;

                if(indegree[neighbour] == 0)
                    q.push(neighbour);
            }
        }

        return topo.size() == numCourses;
    }
};

Complexity Analysis

Time Complexity: (O(V+E)) — Every vertex and edge is processed once.

Space Complexity: (O(V+E)) — Adjacency list, indegree array, queue and topological ordering.
